desc:Sum 92 [summing mixer] (GFX)
//tags: summing mixer cassette four-track desk field location transformer limiter stems bus 92
//author: GardenFX
// by JGWizrad / Garden FX (GFX)
//
// Requires gfx-drift, gfx-output, gfx-ui, gfx-panel, gfx-instrument, gfx-dyn and gfx-faceplate (.jsfx-inc) in the same folder.
//
// SUM 92 - the 92 series summing mixer: 90s domestic, portable and field gear (utilitarian, compact, mobile).
// Three summing mixers in one chassis (four models); the MODEL keys choose which one is in the circuit. Each model is
// the original plug-in ported sample for sample (it nulls against it), with its own controls and faceplate.
//
// SET-UP (REAPER): the bus track needs as many channels as the selected model uses (12 covers every model). Send the
// stems to the channels below and turn off the stem tracks' master / parent send. The mix always leaves on 1/2.
//   15 / 44   (Cassette 4)     8 channels: 1/2 TRACK 1, 3/4 TRACK 2, 5/6 TRACK 3, 7/8 TRACK 4 (stereo stems).
//   12        (Model 12)      12 channels: 1, 2, 3, 4 MONO channels (send to one channel each),
//                                          5/6, 7/8, 9/10, 11/12 STEREO pairs.
//   FIELD     (Field 42 Mix)   8 channels: 1/2, 3/4, 5/6, 7/8 stereo PAIRS (or a mid / side pair: left = mid, right = side).
//   Channels a model does not use are ignored; every output except 1/2 is silenced. The panel shows the map of the
//   selected model on the SET-UP line at the bottom.
//
//   15     CASSETTE 4, MODEL 15 - the compact standard-speed four-track: B-type noise reduction (it lifts the quiet
//          highs on the way in and takes them back down on the way out), a soft top end (~9.5 kHz), a fat head bump,
//          more wow and hiss.
//   44     CASSETTE 4, MODEL 44 - the double-speed machine with the 2:1 / 1:2 compander noise reduction: everything is
//          compressed 2:1 (with a treble lift) onto the tape and expanded 1:2 coming back, so the hiss all but
//          disappears - but the expander reacts to what comes OFF the tape, so it breathes and pumps a little.
//          More top end (~15 kHz), steadier transport.
//          (15 and 44 share one panel and one set of controls, as on the original Cassette 4. Each stem is recorded
//          to its own track of the tape, played back through the heads and mixed down on the recorder's mixer.)
//          TAPE   DRIVE (recording level: hotter = more saturation, level-matched), WOW (one transport: all four
//                 tracks wobble together), HISS (the middle = the model's own; each track has its own hiss),
//                 AGE (a worn tape: the top end goes, dropouts creep in), NR in / out, MASTER.
//          MIXER  per track: TREBLE (10 kHz) and BASS (100 Hz) +/-10 dB, PAN, LEVEL, SOLO, MUTE; VU meters.
//   12     MODEL 12 - the little 12-channel desk from every 90s project studio (it replaces the former Compact 12): clean and honest, with the tight,
//          slightly forward sound of its op-amps - and the gritty edge when you run it hot.
//          CH 1 - 4 (mono): HI (12 kHz), MID (2.5 kHz), LOW (80 Hz), PAN, LEVEL.  CH 5 - 12 (stereo pairs): HI, LOW,
//          BALANCE, LEVEL.  Every channel: SOLO (the SOLO light on the master flashes while anything is soloed), MUTE.
//          MASTER: MAIN MIX, HOT (drives the summing amps into their rails: grit, edge, a little bite; level-matched),
//          NOISE (the desk's own hiss floor); 12-LED meters (0 = 0 VU = -18 dBFS).
//   FIELD  FIELD 42 MIX - the Field 42 channel as a four-pair, battery-powered location mixer. Each pair: HPF (80 - 240
//          Hz, OFF fully left), TRIM into a transformer-balanced preamp, the input safety limiter (LIM), Ø, M/S (the
//          pair is a mid / side mic: left = mid, right = side, and PAN becomes WIDTH; Ø flips the side), PAN
//          (constant loudness, +3 dB at the sides), FADER, MUTE, SOLO. MASTER: the master fader, the output limiter
//          (OFF / ON / LINK, +4 to +20 dBu), the output transformer (IRON), a line-up TONE (1 kHz / 400 Hz / 100 Hz,
//          -20 to +8 dBu), and DRIFT. The VU reads the mix before the master fader; the LEDs read the master output.
//          Levels: 0 dBu = -20 dBFS = 0 VU.
//          DRIFT (50% = standard): battery sag. The supply wanders slowly and droops under heavy, sustained level (the
//          whole mix draws on one battery), so the preamps' headroom, the limiter thresholds and the transformers'
//          drive all move a little. 0% = a fresh battery, 100% = twice the standard amount. (Only FIELD drifts.)
//          Parameter list only: FIELD input iron, limiter attack / release.
//
// OUTPUT: every model's output clips softly at -0.3 dBFS (the house CLIP at its ceiling, always on, no latency, no
//   controls): part of the 92 chassis. Below about -2 dBFS it does nothing. 15 / 44 / 12 play 6 dB quieter than
//   their originals with the same colour (6 dB more headroom before the clip); FIELD plays at its original level.
//
// MODEL SWITCH: only the selected model runs (CPU = that model). A change crossfades the outgoing model into the
//   incoming one over 20 ms (both run for those 20 ms); the incoming model starts from a clean, reset state.
//   (15 <-> 44 also crossfades: each has its own tape engine; they share their settings.)
// LATENCY: 2 ms + 2 samples (98 samples at 48 kHz), reported to the host, the same on every model: the 15 and 44 tapes
//   run through their wow / flutter delay lines (centred there), and the 12 and FIELD hear every input through a plain
//   delay of the same length, so a parallel copy lines up and the MODEL crossfade stays in step.
// PRESETS: the house preset bar. Every preset of the three originals, named by model ("12: Hot Desk"); choosing
//   one also selects its model. "Init" = the 15 at its defaults. "Reset all to defaults" (in the list) resets every
//   model, the MODEL and the output stage (channel names are kept). Presets leave the output stage alone.
// NAMES: click the name at the top of a channel strip (15 / 44 and 12) and type (e.g. Drums); Enter keeps it,
//   Escape cancels, an empty name goes back to the number. The names are saved with the project.
// GUI: drag a knob (SHIFT = fine, double-click = default, wheel); click a key; click the LCD (or PRESETS) for the
//   preset list. S / M / L / ? = size and help. The 15 / 44 and 12 faceplates are shown enlarged to fill the
//   chassis (the same layout as the originals, bigger); FIELD is shown at its own size.

import gfx-drift.jsfx-inc
import gfx-output.jsfx-inc
import gfx-ui.jsfx-inc
import gfx-panel.jsfx-inc
import gfx-instrument.jsfx-inc
import gfx-dyn.jsfx-inc
import gfx-faceplate.jsfx-inc

